IPFS (InterPlanetary File System) is a peer-to-peer, decentralized protocol for storing and sharing files. 
In Web3 and NFT development, it solves a major problem: storing large files (like images, videos, or JSON metadata) directly on a blockchain like Ethereum is prohibitively expensive, 
while storing them on a centralized server (like AWS or Google Drive) creates a single point of failure where the server owner can delete or alter the file.


Core Concept: Content Addressing vs. Location Addressing
The traditional web (HTTP) uses location-based addressing—a URL like [https://example.com/dog.png](https://example.com/dog.png) tells your browser where to look (a specific server). If that server goes offline or the owner swaps dog.png for a picture of a cat, the link breaks or shows different data.



IPFS uses content-based addressing:

When you upload a file to IPFS, the protocol runs its bytes through a cryptographic hash function (typically SHA-256) to generate a unique fingerprint called a Content Identifier (CID) (e.g., ipfs://QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco).

You ask the network what file you want by its CID rather than where it lives.

Tamper-proof: If even a single pixel in an image or a single letter in a JSON file changes, the cryptographic hash changes completely, producing an entirely new CID. Because a smart contract stores that immutable CID, nobody can silently swap out your NFT's artwork.




How IPFS Works Under the Hood:

IPFS combines several subsystems to store, locate, and transfer data across peers:Chunking & Merkle DAGs: Large files are split into smaller blocks (typically 256 KB each). Each chunk gets its own hash, and those hashes are linked together in a tree-like structure called a Merkle DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) under IPLD. The root hash of that graph becomes the file's overall CID.Finding Files (Kademlia DHT): Because there is no central directory, IPFS nodes maintain a Distributed Hash Table across the network that maps CIDs to the IP addresses of peers currently holding those blocks.Transferring Data (Bitswap): Once your node finds peers holding the chunks you need, it uses a BitTorrent-like protocol called Bitswap to download pieces simultaneously from multiple peers and verify each chunk against its hash.






Pinning and Persistence (Crucial Distinction):



A common misconception is that uploading to IPFS automatically stores your file forever across the entire network.

Garbage Collection: By default, when another IPFS node fetches your file, it caches a copy temporarily. To save disk space, nodes periodically run garbage collection and delete unpinned cached files. If every node holding a file deletes it or goes offline, that CID becomes unreachable.

Pinning: To keep a file permanently available, at least one active node must "pin" it—flagging the CID so garbage collection never deletes it. You can do this by running your own 24/7 IPFS node, using a pinning service (such as Pinata, Filebase, or web3.storage), or using Filecoin (an incentivized storage layer built on top of IPFS where storage providers are paid to keep data pinned).





How IPFS Connects to NFTs & Smart Contracts:
In a typical ERC-721 NFT project (like the one built in Patrick Collins' Foundry course), hosting an NFT takes a two-step IPFS upload:

Upload the Image: You upload pug.png to IPFS and get its image CID (ipfs://QmImageHash...).

Upload the Metadata JSON: You create a JSON file following the ERC-721 metadata standard that embeds that image URI:

{
  "name": "Pug #1",
  "description": "An adorable pug stored on IPFS",
  "image": "ipfs://QmImageHash...",
  "attributes": [{ "trait_type": "Cuteness", "value": 100 }]
}


Store on-chain: Your Solidity smart contract stores only that lightweight string (ipfs://QmMetadataHash...) and returns it when someone calls tokenURI(tokenId)




