the issue with IPFS VS HTTPS (THE SOLUTION IS ON CHAIN SVGS)


The Bottom Tier: HTTPS (>>>>>>> HTTPS)

How it works: The tokenURI in your smart contract points to a traditional centralized server (like AWS, Google Cloud, or a project's private API: [https://api.coolcats.com/1](https://api.coolcats.com/1)).

The Issue (Rug-pull & Link Rot): 
Whoever owns that server or domain can change the image at any time (turning a rare NFT into a blank square), forget to renew the domain, or shut the company down. Once the server goes offline, the NFT's metadata is gone forever.




The Middle Tier: IPFS

How it works: Instead of a server location, the tokenURI stores a cryptographic hash (ipfs://Qm...) of your JSON metadata and image.

Why it beats HTTPS: It is immutable (content-addressed). Nobody can silently swap the image behind that hash without changing the hash itself—and since the hash is locked in the smart contract, the artwork cannot be tampered with.

The Catch (Patrick's main point in this video): IPFS is not a blockchain, and it has no built-in financial incentive to keep storing your files.

For an IPFS file to stay accessible, at least one IPFS node in the world has to keep it "pinned" on their hard drive.

If you pin it via Pinata and stop paying (or Pinata shuts down), and nobody else on the network has pinned your specific CID, your file gets garbage-collected and disappears from the network.

While anyone with a local backup can re-pin it to restore the exact same CID, it still relies on off-chain storage hardware staying online. (This is why protocols like Filecoin and Arweave were invented—to add economic incentives to decentralized storage.)


